  summary: [
    "Biodiversity operates at three linked levels &mdash; ecosystem diversity, species diversity and genetic diversity &mdash; and conserving one level usually helps conserve the others too.",
    "Species diversity combines species richness (number of species) and species evenness (how equally abundant they are); Simpson&rsquo;s reciprocal index, D = N(N&minus;1) &divide; &Sigma;n(n&minus;1), combines both into one comparable value &mdash; a high D indicates a stable, even community, a low D indicates dominance by one species and possible disturbance.",
    "Of an estimated ~9 million species on Earth, only around 1.8 million have been formally described, over half of them insects; fossil evidence of past biodiversity is inherently incomplete, since fossilisation is rare and some organisms (e.g. soft-bodied ones) fossilise poorly.",
    "Earth has already experienced five natural mass extinctions (each killing more than 75% of species); scientists believe human activity is now driving a sixth, anthropogenic mass extinction, illustrated by recent extinctions such as the North Island giant moa, the Caribbean monk seal and the Falkland Islands wolf.",
    "Tropical rainforests and coral reefs cover only a small fraction of Earth&rsquo;s surface but hold roughly half of its species, so disturbance such as logging and oil palm conversion (e.g. Borneo&rsquo;s dipterocarp forests) or warming-driven coral bleaching (e.g. the Great Barrier Reef) causes disproportionate biodiversity loss.",
    "The current biodiversity crisis has several interacting anthropogenic causes: exponential human population growth; overexploitation (e.g. overfishing of North Atlantic cod); urbanisation; deforestation and land conversion for agriculture and mining; pollution and disease; and invasive alien species (e.g. lionfish, water hyacinth).",
    "An alien species (introduced by human activity outside its natural range) is not automatically invasive; it becomes invasive only once it increases rapidly in number and measurably harms native species and ecosystems.",
    "In situ conservation protects species within their natural habitat; how effective a protected area is depends on biogeographic factors described by island biogeography theory &mdash; larger, rounder, better-connected reserves support higher species diversity and suffer fewer edge effects.",
    "Rewilding restores natural processes and missing species (e.g. reintroducing grey wolves to Yellowstone), bringing benefits including increased carbon storage, help for species adapting to climate change, and economic and health benefits for local communities.",
    "Ex situ conservation (zoos, botanical gardens, germ plasm and tissue banks) preserves species outside their natural habitat; case studies such as the golden lion tamarin and Bengal tiger show that combining in situ and ex situ methods, tailored to each species&rsquo; own ecology, is usually the most effective strategy.",
    "International agreements such as the 1992 Rio Earth Summit&rsquo;s Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) set global biodiversity targets, reviewed periodically through a Conference of the Parties (CoP).",
    "Phylogenetic diversity measures the evolutionary history represented by a set of species; the EDGE of Existence programme prioritises species that are both Evolutionarily Distinct (above-median ED score) and Globally Endangered (threatened on the IUCN Red List), because losing such a species destroys an entire, irreplaceable branch of the tree of life."
  ]
